  faqs: [
    { q: 'How do I file a Cyber Bureau complaint in Nepal?', a: 'Open the official complaint page at cyberbureau.nepalpolice.gov.np/report-cyber-crime, download the form matching your incident, attach a copy of your identity document plus URLs and clear screenshots, and submit it at a police office, the Cyber Bureau itself, or by email.' },
    { q: 'Can I submit a cybercrime complaint online?', a: 'There is no online complaint-filing portal as of October 2026. The official site provides downloadable forms and instructions; if you cannot attend in person, you can email your complete complaint and attachments to cyberbureau@nepalpolice.gov.np.' },
    { q: 'What documents are required for a Cyber Bureau complaint?', a: 'The application on the official form (typed or handwritten, English or Nepali or both) plus, mandatorily, a copy of the complainant ID (citizenship, national ID, passport, or driving licence) and clear URLs and screenshots. For children, a birth registration certificate copy is listed instead.' },
    { q: 'Can I report cybercrime from outside Kathmandu?', a: 'Yes. The official instructions say complaints can be handed in at the nearest district, area, or ward police office, or a police circle or station across Nepal, not only in Kathmandu.' },
    { q: 'What is the official Cyber Bureau email address?', a: 'cyberbureau@nepalpolice.gov.np. Treat lookalike addresses as fake, and do not send documents to any other address.' },
    { q: 'Where is the Nepal Police Cyber Bureau office?', a: 'At Bhotahity, Kathmandu, under Nepal Police Headquarters. Detailed opening hours are not published on the complaint or contact pages, so confirm visit timing by phone before travelling far.' },
    { q: 'How do I report online banking fraud in Nepal?', a: 'Use the official Online Financial Fraud complaint form, contact your bank or wallet provider immediately through official channels, and call extension 108 (01-5319044) for follow-up. Extensions for status questions are described as office-hours lines.' },
    { q: 'How do I complain about a hacked Facebook account?', a: 'Use the Fake/Hacked ID complaint form for police, and also report to Facebook. The Cyber Bureau notes that reporting through the usual logged-in phone may close faster and suggests answering "No" to the "Do you have a Facebook account?" question in Facebook\'s imposter-report form. Extension 112 handles hacked or fake ID follow-up.' },
    { q: 'Can I report a fake social media account to the Cyber Bureau?', a: 'Yes. Fake and hacked accounts share the same official complaint form and the same follow-up extension (112), and you should also use the platform\'s own imposter or fake-account reporting tools.' },
    { q: 'How can I follow up on my complaint?', a: 'Keep your registration number (if one was issued), a copy of the application, and the submission date, then call 01-5319044 during office hours — extension 108 for online-fraud cases, 109 for whether a complaint is registered, 112 for hacked/fake IDs, and 114 if you emailed a complaint and have not received a registration number. A 24×7 duty line (+977 9851286770) is also listed for general inquiries.' },
    { q: 'Is there a fee for filing a cybercrime complaint?', a: 'No fee is mentioned on the official complaint instructions or contact pages. Confirm current practice directly with the Bureau, and never pay an unofficial agent who claims to speed up registration.' },
    { q: 'What should I do if I do not have the suspect\'s real name?', a: 'The official instructions ask you to provide available details as far as possible — profile and post URLs, account IDs, phone numbers, screenshots, and payment receipts. Digital traces can support police action even when the real name is unknown.' },
    { q: 'Can a minor report cybercrime in Nepal?', a: 'The official document list substitutes a birth registration certificate copy for the standard ID in the case of children. The Bureau does not state age rules publicly, so a parent or guardian should normally support a minor\'s complaint.' },
    { q: 'What should I do if someone threatens to publish my private photos?', a: 'Preserve all chats and evidence, do not pay or negotiate, and file with the Bureau. Use the obscene-content form if the material has already been posted; otherwise describe the facts in the application (the "Other" form may fit). Report the account to the platform as well, and seek immediate police help if you feel unsafe.' },
  ],
